// 04 · the row — a Boolean algebra over effects
//
// Every function carries an effect row: the set of effects its body may
// perform, with four operators over it — `+` union, `-` difference, `&`
// intersection, and `!` negation. The row is inferred from the body; when you
// write one in the signature, you are stating a CONSTRAINT the medium checks
// against what it inferred, not a contract it takes on faith.
//
// The algebra is structural. The compiler does not know what `Alloc` means; a
// handler gives it meaning at runtime. What the row proves is a property over
// that handler — and the most valuable property is a negative one. `!Alloc`
// proves this code allocates nothing, and it proves it TRANSITIVELY: any callee
// that allocates breaks the claim. That is the one thing review can never do
// and a proof always can. (This is the rows-with-negation of Koka's effect
// system, extended with the Boolean negation Koka leaves out — see PLAN §4③.)
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/04-row.mn   ← prints 42
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/04-row.mn:30     ← the cursor on a row; the Ledger unrolls it
import io

// Pure — the empty row. The body performs nothing, and saying so unlocks the
// medium's freedoms: memoize it, evaluate it at compile time, run it in
// parallel with anything. Perform an effect in this body and it fails at
// compile time with E_PurityViolated.
fn scale(x) with Pure = x * 2

// !Alloc — proven to touch no heap. Real-time DSP declares this so a sample
// callback can never stall on an allocation. The body is arithmetic and
// branches only; nothing here builds a value on the heap, so the claim holds.
fn bound(x, lo, hi) with !Alloc = if x < lo { lo } else if x > hi { hi } else { x }

// A negation names a DECLARED effect. The program says what the network IS —
// one capability with its operations — and a clause naming an effect no
// declaration backs is refused (E_MissingVariable): a negation of nothing
// would prove nothing.
effect Network {
  fetch(url: String) -> String
}

// !Network — anything EXCEPT the network. The negation subtracts that one
// capability from the universe of allowed effects. Load a function proven
// `!Network` into an untrusted chain and it cannot reach the network, whatever
// else it does. The proof is the sandbox; there is no runtime firewall to trust.
fn local_only(x) with !Network = bound(x, 0, 100)

fn main() = {
  // Pure: 42
  let y = scale(21)
  // !Network, clamped to 100
  let z = local_only(200)
  y |> int_to_str |> print_string
}
